## Built-in alternative

For simple cases, the platform already does this:

```javascript
const params = new URLSearchParams('?name=John&age=30');
params.get('name');   // 'John'
params.getAll('tag'); // ['a', 'b', 'c']
Object.fromEntries(params); // { name: 'John', age: '30' }
```

`URLSearchParams` is faster and battle-tested, but it returns a `URLSearchParams` instance rather than a plain object, and it throws on malformed percent-encoding when you call `get()` — so the manual version above is handy when you need a plain object with lenient parsing.

## A robust `parseQueryString`

```js
/**
 * Parse a query string into a plain object.
 *
 * @param {string} queryString  e.g. "?name=Ada&tags=js&tags=node&q=hello+world"
 * @returns {Record<string, string | string[]>}
 */
function parseQueryString(queryString) {
  const params = {};

  if (typeof queryString !== 'string') return params;

  // Strip a leading "?" and any "#fragment"
  let qs = queryString.trim();
  if (qs.startsWith('?')) qs = qs.slice(1);
  const hashIndex = qs.indexOf('#');
  if (hashIndex !== -1) qs = qs.slice(0, hashIndex);

  if (!qs) return params;

  // "+" means space in application/x-www-form-urlencoded
  const decode = (str) => {
    try {
      return decodeURIComponent(str.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
    } catch {
      return str; // malformed percent-encoding: keep the raw text
    }
  };

  for (const pair of qs.split('&')) {
    if (!pair) continue; // skip empty segments like "a=1&&b=2"

    const eq = pair.indexOf('=');
    const rawKey = eq === -1 ? pair : pair.slice(0, eq);
    const rawValue = eq === -1 ? '' : pair.slice(eq + 1);

    const key = decode(rawKey);
    const value = decode(rawValue);

    if (!key) continue;

    // Guard against prototype pollution
    if (key === '__proto__' || key === 'constructor' || key === 'prototype') continue;

    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(params, key)) {
      // Repeated key -> collect into an array
      params[key] = Array.isArray(params[key])
        ? [...params[key], value]
        : [params[key], value];
    } else {
      params[key] = value;
    }
  }

  return params;
}
```

### Examples

```js
parseQueryString('?name=Ada&role=engineer');
// { name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer' }

parseQueryString('tags=js&tags=node&tags=web');
// { tags: ['js', 'node', 'web'] }

parseQueryString('q=hello+world&note=100%25%20done');
// { q: 'hello world', note: '100% done' }

parseQueryString('flag&other=');
// { flag: '', other: '' }

parseQueryString('?a=1#section');
// { a: '1' }   ← fragment removed
```

### Notes on the edge cases handled

| Case | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Leading `?` | Optional, stripped |
| `#fragment` | Removed before parsing |
| `+` | Decoded as a space |
| `%20`, `%E2%9C%93` | URI-decoded (per component, not the whole string) |
| Repeated keys | Collected into an array |
| Key with no `=` | Value becomes `''` |
| Empty segments (`&&`) | Skipped |
| Bad percent-encoding (`%E0%A4%A`) | Raw text kept instead of throwing |
| `__proto__` / `constructor` / `prototype` | Ignored (prototype-pollution guard) |

### Built-in alternative

Modern browsers and Node have `URLSearchParams`, which handles most of this for you:

```js
const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
const name = params.get('name');
const tags = params.getAll('tags'); // ['js', 'node']
```

If you want an object from it, note that `Object.fromEntries` keeps only the *last* value for repeated keys:

```js
Object.fromEntries(new URLSearchParams('a=1&a=2')); // { a: '2' }
```

To keep all values, reduce manually:

```js
const obj = {};
for (const [k, v] of new URLSearchParams(qs)) {
  (obj[k] ??= []).push(v);
}
```

Use the hand-written version if you need the array behavior, prototype-pollution protection, or support for very old environments.

## Modern approach (uses the built-in `URLSearchParams`)

